const RAW=[
['dal','Dal Lake','Srinagar','lake',['Nature','Photography','Culture'],[34.11,74.87],'Srinagar’s lake of shikaras, floating gardens and houseboats.','Dal Lake is less a single lake than a living neighbourhood: houseboats, vegetable gardens and boat lanes share the water.|Go at dawn when mist sits low and boatmen call across the still surface. Ask your boatman to take the quiet back channels.','Dal Lake lies in Srinagar and is known for shikaras and houseboats.','Locals speak of floating gardens that drift in the mist. Treat this as folklore.'],
['nis','Nishat Bagh','Srinagar','garden',['History','Nature'],[34.13,74.87],'Terraced Mughal garden stepping down to Dal Lake.','Nishat is often translated as “joy”. Its terraces descend toward the lake so water and views lead you outward.|Walk top to bottom, then look back up. In autumn the chinar trees turn copper.','A Mughal garden on Dal Lake’s eastern shore, commonly dated to the 1630s (verify).',''],
['sha','Shankaracharya Temple','Srinagar','temple',['History','Pilgrimage','Photography'],[34.08,74.84],'Hilltop temple with sweeping views of Srinagar.','Climb the hill and Srinagar opens below you: the lake, the Jhelum and old rooftops.|Check opening times and security rules locally. Phones and cameras may be restricted.','The temple stands on a hill above Srinagar (verify age and history).','Tradition links the site to the philosopher Adi Shankara. Historians differ.'],
['haz','Hazratbal Shrine','Srinagar','shrine',['Culture','Pilgrimage'],[34.13,74.84],'White-domed shrine on Dal Lake’s western shore.','The white dome glows against the lake. For many Kashmiris it is a place of prayer and gathering.|Dress modestly, keep voices low, and follow local guidance.','An important Muslim shrine on Dal Lake’s western shore.','Devotees hold beliefs about a venerated relic here. Present it respectfully.'],
['old','Srinagar Old City','Srinagar','shrine',['Culture','Food','Shopping','History'],[34.10,74.82],'Lanes, wooden shrines and bakeries along the Jhelum.','The Old City is where Kashmir’s crafts live on: papier-mâché, walnut carving, shawls and bakeries that open before dawn.|Follow the river and look up at carved wooden balconies. A local guide helps with etiquette and bargaining.','The Old City is the historic core along the Jhelum.',''],
['jam','Jamia Masjid','Srinagar','shrine',['History','Culture'],[34.10,74.81],'Grand wooden mosque with a shaded courtyard.','Its courtyard is ringed by arches and shaded by trees, a quiet pause from the bazaar.|Rows of tall wooden pillars hold up the prayer hall. Visit outside prayer times and check visitor rules.','Founded in the late 14th century under Sultan Sikandar (verify).',''],
['hari','Hari Parbat Fort','Srinagar','ruin',['History','Photography'],[34.10,74.83],'Hilltop fort ringed by old walls.','Hari Parbat watches over Srinagar. Shrines of different faiths sit on its slopes, a quiet picture of the city’s layers.|Almond blossom at nearby Badamwari draws visitors in spring.','Walls date to Akbar’s era; the present fort is linked to Afghan rule (verify).','Local tradition ties the hill to a goddess. Accounts vary.'],
['pari','Pari Mahal','Srinagar','garden',['History','Photography'],[34.08,74.87],'Terraced garden-ruin above Dal Lake.','Arched terraces climb the slope with the lake spread below. It is a favourite sunset spot.|Bring water for the short climb.','A terraced Mughal-era structure overlooking Dal Lake (verify date and builder).','The name means “palace of fairies”. Fairy tales are folklore.'],
['cha','Chashme Shahi','Srinagar','garden',['History','Nature'],[34.09,74.88],'Small royal garden built around a spring.','Chashme Shahi means “royal spring”. Its source feeds terraced channels and fountains.|Compact and peaceful. Pair it with Pari Mahal.','A Mughal garden around a natural spring, dated to the 1630s (verify).','Locals say the spring water has healing powers. This is unproven.'],
['shal','Shalimar Bagh','Srinagar','garden',['History','Nature','Photography'],[34.15,74.87],'Grand Mughal garden north of Dal Lake.','Shalimar is laid out along a central canal with terraces and chinar trees, among Kashmir’s grandest gardens.|Arrive in the afternoon for the best light on the chinars.','A Mughal garden of the early 17th century linked to Jahangir (verify).','A romantic tradition says it was a gift for his wife.'],
['tul','Tulip Garden','Srinagar','garden',['Nature','Photography'],[34.09,74.86],'Terraced tulip garden below the Zabarwan hills.','In spring, rows of tulips in many colours bloom on terraces facing the lake.|The season is short and weather-dependent. Check bloom dates before planning.','Indira Gandhi Memorial Tulip Garden opened in 2007 and runs a short spring season (verify).',''],
['dach','Dachigam National Park','Srinagar','forest',['Nature','Adventure'],[34.13,74.99],'Forest sanctuary home to the hangul.','Dachigam shelters the hangul, or Kashmir stag, a rare deer found in this region.|Visits are regulated, so check permits. Go early, and remember sightings are never guaranteed.','Dachigam is a national park near Srinagar and key habitat of the hangul.',''],
['gul','Gulmarg','Baramulla','snow',['Nature','Adventure','Photography'],[34.05,74.38],'Alpine meadow and ski resort in the Pir Panjal.','Gulmarg’s name is often read as “meadow of flowers”. Summer carpets it in wildflowers, winter turns it into a well-known ski area.|The Gondola cable car climbs toward Apharwat. Book ahead and watch the weather, since wind can pause rides.','Gulmarg is a hill resort in Baramulla district with a well-known cable car.',''],
['pah','Pahalgam','Anantnag','meadow',['Nature','Adventure','Pilgrimage'],[34.02,75.32],'River valley of pine forests beside the Lidder.','Pahalgam sits beside the Lidder River, ringed by pine forest. Pony rides, riverside picnics and quiet walks make it a slow-travel favourite.|It is also a starting point for the Amarnath Yatra, so crowds and rules change by season.','Pahalgam is a hill town on the Lidder River in Anantnag district.',''],
['bet','Betaab Valley','Anantnag','meadow',['Nature','Photography'],[34.07,75.35],'Green valley named after a film.','Betaab Valley takes its name from the 1983 film Betaab, shot here. Streams cut across the meadow beneath pine slopes.|Entry and local transport are regulated, so ask your driver about timings.','Named after the 1983 film Betaab, filmed here.',''],
['aru','Aru Valley','Anantnag','meadow',['Nature','Adventure'],[34.09,75.26],'Village meadow and trek base above Pahalgam.','Aru is quieter than Pahalgam. Shepherd huts, open meadow and peaks suit short treks and photography.|It is a calm place to adjust before higher treks.','Aru is a village near Pahalgam and a trekking base.',''],
['son','Sonamarg','Ganderbal','snow',['Nature','Adventure','Photography'],[34.30,75.29],'Meadow of gold beneath glaciers.','Sonamarg means “meadow of gold”, often explained by flowers glowing in sunlight. The Sindh river runs through, with Thajiwas Glacier above.|Ponies carry visitors toward the glacier. Check road and weather advisories first, as the route toward Zojila can close.','Sonamarg is in Ganderbal district on the route toward Zojila.',''],
['kheer','Kheer Bhawani','Ganderbal','temple',['Pilgrimage','Culture'],[34.19,74.79],'Sacred spring temple among chinars.','A temple built around a sacred spring in a chinar grove at Tulmulla. A major festival draws devotees each year.|Visitors should be respectful and follow temple rules.','Kheer Bhawani is a Hindu temple and spring at Tulmulla, Ganderbal.','Devotees say the spring water changes colour as an omen, a widely told belief.'],
['man','Manasbal Lake','Ganderbal','lake',['Nature','Photography'],[34.24,74.67],'Quiet lotus lake north of Srinagar.','Manasbal is far calmer than Dal. In summer lotus flowers cover parts of the lake and birds gather at the edges.|Hire a rowing boat and stay for sunset.','Manasbal is a freshwater lake in Ganderbal, often described as one of Kashmir’s deepest (verify).',''],
['wul','Wular Lake','Bandipora','lake',['Nature','Photography'],[34.37,74.55],'Vast freshwater lake near Bandipora.','Wular is often described as among Asia’s largest freshwater lakes. Its size shifts with seasons and wind can raise waves quickly.|Best from the shore at sunset. Boats depend on weather.','Wular Lake is in Bandipora district and is a major wetland.',''],
['gur','Gurez Valley','Bandipora','snow',['Nature','Adventure','Culture'],[34.64,74.84],'Remote Himalayan valley beside the Kishanganga.','Gurez is a remote valley with wooden villages and open meadows. Habba Khatoon peak rises over the settlements.|Access is seasonal and visitor rules apply. Verify permits and road status before planning.','Gurez is a remote valley in Bandipora district. Permits may apply (verify).','Locals link Habba Khatoon peak to the poet queen of the same name.'],
['lol','Lolab Valley','Kupwara','meadow',['Nature','Culture'],[34.53,74.35],'Green valley of orchards and forests.','Lolab is known for orchards, forests and meadows, with villages rooted in farming.|Quieter than the famous valleys. Local guides help with routes.','Lolab is a valley in Kupwara district.',''],
['ban','Bangus Valley','Kupwara','meadow',['Nature','Adventure'],[34.42,74.42],'Wide open meadow valley in Kupwara.','Bangus is a broad, open meadow ringed by forest and peaks, with far fewer visitors than Gulmarg.|Go with a local guide, check access rules, and carry food and water.','Bangus is a meadow valley in Kupwara district (verify access rules).',''],
['dood','Doodhpathri','Budgam','meadow',['Nature','Photography'],[33.87,74.57],'Pale stream through green meadows.','The name means “valley of milk”, often linked to the pale foam of the Shaliganga stream.|A popular, calm day trip from Srinagar.','Doodhpathri is a meadow in Budgam district.',''],
['yus','Yusmarg','Budgam','meadow',['Nature','Photography'],[33.82,74.67],'Pine meadow in the Pir Panjal foothills.','Yusmarg is an open meadow among pines, a gentler outing than the higher resorts.|Streams and short walks suit families.','Yusmarg is a meadow in Budgam district.','A popular tradition ties the name to Prophet Jesus. It is unverified.'],
['awan','Awantipora Ruins','Pulwama','ruin',['History','Photography'],[33.92,75.02],'Ruined 9th-century temples by the Jhelum.','Two ruined stone temples stand near the Jhelum, built under King Avantivarman. Carved details survive in places.|A small site. Pair it with other Anantnag-area sights.','Ruins of temples associated with King Avantivarman, 9th century (verify).',''],
['mar','Martand Sun Temple','Anantnag','ruin',['History','Photography'],[33.75,75.25],'Grand ruined Sun temple on a plateau.','Martand’s massive stone ruins crown a plateau above the valley, showing Kashmir’s architectural ambition.|Visit in morning light for photography.','An 8th-century temple associated with the Karkota dynasty and Lalitaditya (verify).',''],
['ver','Verinag','Anantnag','garden',['History','Nature'],[33.54,75.25],'Octagonal spring pool at the Jhelum’s source.','Verinag is regarded as the source of the Jhelum. An arcaded octagonal basin holds clear spring water.|The Mughal emperor Jahangir’s love of Kashmir shaped the garden around it.','A spring and Mughal-era garden complex in Anantnag associated with Jahangir (verify).',''],
['ach','Achabal Garden','Anantnag','garden',['History','Nature'],[33.71,75.30],'Mughal garden fed by a mountain spring.','Achabal’s fountains run from a spring at the head of the garden. Chinars and flowing water keep summer afternoons cool.|A good picnic stop on an Anantnag route.','A Mughal garden with a natural spring in Anantnag district (verify).',''],
['aha','Aharbal Waterfall','Kulgam','falls',['Nature','Adventure'],[33.66,74.80],'Powerful waterfall in a forested gorge.','The Veshav stream drops in a loud cascade. Mist and forest make it Kulgam’s most dramatic stop.|Stay behind safety barriers and avoid wet rock.','Aharbal is a waterfall in Kulgam district.','']
];
